/** @page jni_use_java_in_qoreguide Using Java APIs in Qore

    @tableofcontents

    @section jniimport Importing Java APIs into Qore

    To import Java APIs into the %Qore program; any of the following jni-module-specific parse directives can be used,
    each of which also causes the jni module to be loaded and initialized:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) import java.namespace.path.* @endcode imports the given wilcard path or class into
      the %Qore program
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) add-classpath filesystem/path:another/path @endcode adds the given paths to the
      Program-specific runtime dynamic classpath; supports environment variable substitution
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) add-relative-classpath ../relative/path @endcode adds the given paths as relative
      to the current program to the Program-specific runtime dynamic classpath
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) global-add-classpath filesystem/path:another/path @endcode adds the given paths to the
      global runtime dynamic classpath; supports environment variable substitution
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) global-add-relative-classpath ../relative/path @endcode adds the given paths as relative
      to the current program to the global runtime dynamic classpath
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) register-shared-class qore.Namespace.ClassName @endcode gives an exact dynamically
      projected %Qore class one Java identity across sibling %Program classloaders; the first registering %Program
      owns the Java class and every later registrant delegates to it

    A classpath with a NUL character is refused with \c JNI-ERROR by the classpath commands above (and with
    \c JDBC-OPTION-ERROR as the \c classpath option of the \c jdbc driver), as a file path cannot have one.

    The entries of a classpath are separated by the system path separator (\c ":" on UNIX) or by \c ";"; each entry
    is a directory, a \c .jar or \c .zip file, or a pattern with \c "*" in its last path element, which is the
    \c .jar and \c .zip files in its directory that match the pattern (ex: <tt>lib/</tt><tt>*.jar</tt>); a wildcard
    in a directory element is not supported.  Only \c "*" is a wildcard, which matches any sequence of characters;
    every other character of the pattern matches itself, also one that is special in a regular expression, so that
    <tt>a[</tt><tt>*.jar</tt> matches \c a[1.jar, and <tt>a.</tt><tt>*.jar</tt> matches \c a.1.jar but not
    \c aX1.jar.  A relative entry of the
    \c add-classpath and \c global-add-classpath module commands, of the \c classpath option of the \c jdbc driver
    and of the \c QORE_JNI_CLASSPATH environment variable is resolved against the JVM's \c user.dir, which is
    normally the current directory of the process when the JVM is started, not the current directory of the process
    when the classpath is added; this includes a wildcard with no directory part, so that \c *.jar is the jar files
    in \c user.dir.  The path of the \c add-relative-classpath and \c global-add-relative-classpath module commands is
    relative to the directory of the Program's script (or to the current directory of the process if the Program has
    no script directory), so that \c *.jar is the jar files there.
    When a sandbox applies a filesystem policy to \c add-classpath or the JDBC \c classpath option, it checks
    a bare wildcard against the JVM's \c user.dir.

    An entry that does not exist is left out, as are other entries that cannot be used (ex: a wildcard whose
    directory does not exist or cannot be read, or a file that is not a \c .jar or \c .zip file), which are
    reported on the console.  An error while a classpath is added (a Java
    exception raised by the class loader) is raised to the caller as \c JNI-CLASSPATH-ERROR, whose description names
    the input that the classpath comes from and the classpath: the classpath commands above raise it, as does the
    \c classpath option of the \c jdbc driver when a connection is opened, and the module's initialization fails
    with it for the \c QORE_JNI_CLASSPATH environment variable (\c MODULE-INIT-ERROR when the module is loaded, or
    the error itself when a %Program requires the loaded module or uses a jni API that creates its jni data).

    All classes in \c java.lang.* are imported implicitly.  Referencing an imported class in %Qore code
    causes a %Qore class to be generated dynamically that presents the Java class.   Instantiating a %Qore class
    based on a Java class also instantiates an internal Java object that is attached to the %Qore object.  Calling
    methods on the object will cause the same Java methods to be called with any arguments provided.

    An import is visible only in the @ref Qore::Program "Program" that made it, and in the child Programs that
    inherit that Program's classes when they are created.  Another Program that loads the jni module, whether it is
    created before or after the import, does not see the class until it imports the class itself; code must therefore
    import every Java class that it names outside \c java.lang.*, even if another Program has already imported it.

    @see @ref jni_class_mapping for more information

    @section jni_define_class_parse_time Defining Java Classes at Parse Time in Qore

    The jni module also supports defining classes at parse time with the following jni-module-specific parse
    directives:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) define-class <name> <base 64 bytecode> @endcode defines the given class with
      \c &lt;name&gt; as the Java internal name for the class (ex: \c my/package/MyClassName)
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) define-pending-class <name> <base 64 bytecode> @endcode adds the given class and
      bytecode as a pending class to be defined when required by a \c define-class directive; this should be used for
      inner classes and has been implemented to allow circular dependencies in such classes to be resolved;
      \c &lt;name&gt; is the Java internal name for the inner class (ex: \c my/package/MyClassName$1)

    @section jni_set_property_parse_time Setting Java System Properties at Parse Time in Qore

    The jni module also supports setting Java system properties at parse time with the following jni-module-specific
    parse directive:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(jni) set-property <propname> <value> @endcode sets the given property at parse time

    @section jni_class_mapping Java Class and Package Mapping in Qore

    @subsection jni_package_mapping Java Package to Qore Namespace Mapping

    The \c jni module maps Java packages to %Qore namespaces.  Therefore in Java \c java.lang.String can be referred
    to as \c java::lang::String in Qore as in the following example:
    @code{.py}
    java::lang::String str();
    @endcode

    The \c jni module attempts to create %Qore classes that match the source Java class as closely as possible to
    maximize the usefulness of Java APIs in %Qore.

    When creating %Qore class methods from the corresponding Java source method, types are mapped according to
    @ref jni_java_to_qore.  Because %Qore has fewer types than Java, this can lead to method signature collissions in
    the generated %Qore class.

    In such cases, only the first method returned by the internal call to \c java.lang.Class.getDeclaredMethods() is
    mapped.

    To call an unmapped method, use reflection to get a \c java.lang.Method object and make the call using
    \c java.lang.Method.invoke() as in the folllowing example:
    @code{.py}
    StringBuilder stringBuilder("");
    Method stringBuilderAppendLong = stringBuilderClass.getMethod("append", Long::TYPE);
    stringBuilderAppendLong.invoke(stringBuilder, 20);
    printf("%y\n", stringBuilder.toString());
    @endcode

    @subsection jniclassmapping Java Inner Classes in Qore

    Java inner classes are mapped as %Qore classes by appending \c "__" to the outer class's name and then appending
    the name of the inner class.

    For example:
    @code{.java}
class Outer {
   public Outer() {
   }

   public class Inner {
       public Inner() {
       }
   }
}
    @endcode

    The inner class would be created as \c "Outer__Inner" in %Qore.

    @subsection jni_class_subclassing Subclassing Java Classes in Qore

    When subclassing Java classes in %Qore, the %Qore class code is not executed by Java when dispatching method calls
    in Java itself.  This means that overriding Java methods in %Qore is only valid for code executed from %Qore.

    To override a Java method when called from Java, you must subclass the class in Java as well and import the child
    class into your %Qore program.

    One exception to this limitation is with interfaces; programmers can use the
    @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" class to execute %Qore-language callbacks
    based on Java interfaces; see the documentation for
    @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" for more information.

    @subsection jni_callback_sandbox Qore Callbacks on Java Threads and Sandboxing

    Java can call %Qore code that it has been given - a
    @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" proxy, a closure or call reference
    passed to Java as an @ref org.qore.jni.QoreClosure, or a method of a %Qore object passed to Java - on any thread,
    including a Java thread that %Qore did not start (a Java executor, a listener thread of a messaging client, a
    timer thread).  Such a call runs under the sandbox (\c Qore::SandboxManager) of the code that handed the %Qore
    code to Java: the code that created the \c QoreInvocationHandler, the code that passed the closure or the object
    to Java (each Java wrapper of an object carries the sandbox of the code that created it), or, for an object that
    Java creates, the code that runs the Java constructor.  The release of the %Qore code, when the handler is
    destroyed or Java no longer references the closure, and the destruction of an object that Java destroys, run
    under the same sandbox.  A callback can therefore never do more than the code that registered it, whatever
    thread Java calls it on.

    A call of an instance method of a Java object that another Program owns (ex: an object that a trusted Program
    hands to sandboxed code) enters the object's Program for the call, like a call of a %Qore function of that
    Program: Java uses the object's class map and \c ClassLoader, %Qore code of the object's Program that Java calls
    back during the call uses that Program's thread-local data, the Program's entry handler
    (\c Program::setThreadEntryHandler()) runs once around the whole Java call, and its thread initialization code
    (\c Program::setThreadInit()) runs on a thread's first entry; the entry handler and the thread initialization
    code run in the thread's current context, under the sandbox of the calling code.  The call holds the Program's
    thread count like any other call into the Program, so deleting the Program waits for the Java calls in progress,
    also those made by sandboxed code, which can therefore delay the deletion for as long as the Java method runs.
    The calling code stays visible to the sandbox: %Qore code that Java calls back during the call is governed by the
    caller's sandbox, as it is when the caller calls the same code directly, and so are
    \c org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox queries and captures made by the Java code (see @ref jni_sandbox); the object's
    Program's own sandbox, if it has one, governs its own code that Java calls back.  The call raises
    \c PROGRAM-ERROR, before the Java method is called, if the object's Program is being torn down or is being parsed
    by another thread, and raises any exception of the Program's entry handler.  A call within the object's own
    Program does not enter it again.  A method of a Java class that another Program loaded (ex: a class that a child
    Program inherited) is run by %Qore in that Program, the class's source Program, which %Qore enters for the call;
    a static method then uses the jni context of that Program, and the module does not enter another Program.

    For code that a sandbox governs, Java's calls into %Qore by a Program that Java names (\c org.qore.jni.QoreJavaApi,
    the class loader of a Program, the constructors, static methods, functions and constants of the Java classes
    generated for the %Qore definitions of a Program) are made only in the Program of the %Qore code that called Java,
    whatever Program owns the Java object whose method it called, and are otherwise refused with \c JNI-ERROR: a Java
    object of another Program, and the objects that its methods return, do not give sandboxed code the reach of that
    Program (qore#6845, qore#7023; with a %Qore library before qore#7028, a call of a method of a Java class of
    another Program made by code that the other Program called directly, as a closure, is judged with the Program
    that called that code).  %Qore code that a Program hands to other code as a callback still runs in its own
    Program, as in %Qore: a %Qore closure, a %Qore object (also when Java calls its methods), and an interface that
    the Program implements with a \c QoreInvocationHandler.

    Java threads are restricted too, as their %Qore caller is not known: once code that a sandbox governs has called
    Java in the process, a Java thread that no %Qore code calls (a \c Thread, an \c Executor, a \c Timer, a pool
    thread, ...) reaches no Program by a Program pointer.  Trusted code applies this policy before any sandboxed code
    runs with:
    @code{.py}
%module-cmd(jni) set-java-thread-policy strict
    @endcode
    The command cannot be undone, and code that has restricting parse options, or that a sandbox governs that does
    not trust it with uncontrolled APIs, cannot issue it (code that a sandbox trusts with uncontrolled APIs enables
    the policy anyway when it calls Java).  %Qore objects and closures that Java calls on any thread are callbacks, which run in their own Program and
    are not restricted so; a Program whose work runs on Java threads hands Java a %Qore closure or object, or an
    interface implemented with a \c QoreInvocationHandler.  A Java object used as a %Qore closure is a callback of the
    code that obtained it from Java, and the Java code of a call of a Java object of another sandboxed Program (ex: a
    tenant's object that a host calls) reaches no Program; Java code of the classes of a sandboxed Program's class
    loader reaches only that Program, whatever code runs it, and code that cannot reach a Program cannot change or
    create its class loaders.  An operation on a class loader is checked against the class loader that it changes.
    The classes of a Kotlin script evaluated with \c kotlin_eval() or \c kotlin-eval belong to the Program that
    evaluated it.  A class loader that is not a \c QoreURLClassLoader belongs to no Program, whatever its parent: once
    sandboxed code has used Java, the calls into %Qore of Java code of its classes are refused.

    The Java thread rule also applies to class loaders and compilers.  In a JVM started with %Qore as a library, the
    JVM's main thread can create a \c QoreURLClassLoader; once sandboxed code has used Java, a new thread that Java
    started, with no %Qore caller, cannot.

    @note <b>Known limit:</b> after any call of Java by code that a sandbox governs, trusted Java-started threads can
    no longer add classpaths or build compilers, for the rest of the process.  Threads that %Qore code calls are not
    affected.  The limit is process-wide, so one sandboxed Program can cause it for all trusted Java-started threads;
    it refuses the operation and grants nothing.  The module does not decide per thread origin because that cannot be
    proven safe: work that sandboxed code submits to a shared thread (ex: a common pool or a host's executor) looks
    the same as trusted work there.  A host that needs these operations performs them from %Qore code.  This also
    applies to threads started by a host's interface code or by third-party JARs that it loads, which must not rely
    on these operations once sandboxed code can run in the process.  An explicit grant for trusted worker threads is
    tracked in qoretechnologies/qore#7229.

    @par Closing class loaders and compilers
    Closing a \c QoreURLClassLoader or a compiler is refused with \c JNI-ERROR to code that cannot reach its Program:
    code that a sandbox governs and that belongs to another Program, Java code of a class loader of another sandboxed
    Program or of an unknown class loader, and, once sandboxed code has used Java in the process, a thread that Java
    started and that no %Qore code calls (ex: an \c Executor, a \c Cleaner or a shutdown hook).  This also applies when
    the object belongs to trusted code: such a thread is refused because the Java stack does not reliably identify the
    code that supplied the work that it runs, so the module cannot tell a trusted owner from sandboxed code.

    @par
    A compiler whose close is refused stays open, with its class loader and JAR files, until code that can reach its
    Program closes it or the process exits.  A host that uses compilers alongside sandboxed code therefore closes them
    from the %Qore code that owns them, and not from a Java thread.

    @par
    Once the module's shutdown has started, the caller can no longer be checked: class loaders and compilers are then
    not closed and their compilation caches are not cleared, for any caller, no exception is raised, and the process
    releases their resources when it exits.

    @note Java objects have no Program of their own: when host code runs a Java object or closure that sandboxed code
    supplied or could have built (a listener, a \c Runnable, \c toString(), a closure that it passes on), its calls into
    %Qore are made with the host's authority if it is made only of classes of the JDK or of trusted class loaders.  A
    host must therefore not run Java objects or closures that sandboxed code supplied; it wraps them in a %Qore closure
    that it controls, or does not accept them.  A %Qore object or closure that Java code can find (ex: in a static
    field) can be called with all of its methods, in its own Program; a host must not leave them in Java state that
    sandboxed Java code can reach.

    @warning A sandbox that allows uncontrolled APIs, and so Java, is not an isolation boundary against malicious
    code: Java code has the rights of the JVM, which can read or change any state of the process.  The checks above
    govern Java's calls into %Qore through this module and are defense in depth for code that a sandbox trusts with
    Java; code that must be isolated is not given uncontrolled APIs.

    A call of a Java method or constructor, or of a Java object used as a %Qore closure, sets the thread's Java
    context - the %Qore Program that Java's calls into %Qore on the thread, such as
    \c org.qore.jni.QoreClosure.call(), resolve, together with the thread's context class loader - to the class
    loader of the Program whose jni context it uses, for the Java code that the call runs; the context stays set after
    the call until the next call sets it.  The other functions that run Java code do not enter the Program of the Java
    object they are given, and set the context to the context of the calling code, in which they also convert their
    arguments and results, before any Java code runs (ex: the static initializers that looking up the method of
    \c invoke() can run): \c Jni::org::qore::jni::invoke(), \c invoke_nonvirtual(), \c kotlin_eval(),
    \c kotlin_scripting_available() and \c kotlin_scripting_retry() (whose discovery of the Kotlin script engine uses
    the thread's context class loader), \c JavaArray::toString() and \c JavaArray::deepToString() (of an array of a
    reference type); the \c kotlin-eval module command sets it to the context of the Program being parsed.  The
    lookup of a Java class that is not yet known and the creation of its %Qore class, which initialize the Java class
    and so run its static initializers (ex: in \c load_class(), \c implement_interface() or an import, for a class of
    the JVM's system class path as well as of a Program's class loader), set it to the context of the Program that
    the class is loaded for.  A read of a Java field, also of a static field, or of a \c JavaArray element, made by
    another Program's code too, sets it to the context of the calling code only if converting the value to %Qore runs
    Java code that the value's class can override: the methods of a \c java.util.Map or \c java.util.List,
    \c toString() of a \c java.sql.Timestamp, \c java.sql.Date, \c java.sql.Time, \c java.math.BigDecimal or
    \c microsoft.sql.DateTimeOffset, and \c get() of an \c org.qore.jni.QoreObjectBase or
    \c org.qore.jni.QoreClosure; a read whose value runs no such code (ex: a primitive value or a string) makes no
    further call into Java; writing a field or an array element does not set it.  A call from Java into %Qore through
    \c QoreClosure, \c QoreObject (\c callMethod(), \c getMemberValue()), \c QoreJavaApi (\c callFunction(),
    \c callStaticMethod(), \c newObjectSave()), the \c QoreInvocationHandler of a proxy or a Java class generated
    from a %Qore class (its constructors, destructor, methods, static methods, functions and constants) saves the
    thread's Java context and restores it when it returns to Java, also when an exception is raised, so that the Java
    code that called into %Qore continues with its own context, whatever Programs the %Qore code made Java calls for;
    so does the loading of a \c qore.* or \c qoremod.* class, and the listing of the classes of such a package, as
    either can load a %Qore module and run its initialization code.  A thread that had no %Qore Java context keeps the
    one that the %Qore code set, as Java's calls into %Qore need one, and the class loader of a Program that has been
    deleted meanwhile is not restored.

    A Java object used as a %Qore closure (an object of a class implementing \c org.qore.jni.QoreClosureMarker that
    Java returns to %Qore) is called in the same way in the Program whose code it was returned to, also when
    another Program calls it: that Program is entered for the call, with its entry handler and thread
    initialization code, and the call raises \c PROGRAM-ERROR if the Program is being torn down or is being parsed
    by another thread.

    An object whose Program has no jni context (ex: a Java object of a Java base class), or whose Program has already
    been deleted, so that its jni context has been destroyed, is called in the context of the calling code: the
    jni context of the current Program, or else of the Program of the calling code, or else of the module's global
    Java context Program, is used, and no Program is entered for the call.  This also applies when another thread
    finishes deleting the Program while the call is about to enter it; while the deletion is still in progress, the
    call raises \c PROGRAM-ERROR.

    @subsection jni_sandbox Sandboxing Java I/O

    %Qore enforces the network and filesystem policy of a sandbox (\c Qore::SandboxManager, ex:
    \c SandboxManager::blockPrivateNetworks()) for the I/O that goes through %Qore: sockets, QUIC and file
    operations.  Java code called through this module does its own I/O through the JVM - \c java.net, \c java.nio,
    \c java.io, JDBC drivers and other Java libraries - which %Qore never sees, and the JDK has no in-process
    replacement for the Security Manager (deprecated by JEP 411 and disabled by JEP 486).  A sandboxed caller can
    therefore reach a private network or the filesystem through any Java code path that does not check its I/O
    itself.  Java classes imported through this module are tagged with the \c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API functional
    domain for this reason, so that a Program with \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS cannot use them directly.

    Java code that sandboxed %Qore code may call - for example the actions of a data provider implemented in Java,
    which connect to destinations taken from a tenant's connection - checks every network destination and every
    path it uses with @ref org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox "org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox", which applies the policy of the
    %Qore code that called into Java:
    - <tt>QoreSandbox.checkNetworkAccess(host, port)</tt> resolves the host name once with the %Qore resolver and
      checks every address with the network policy of %Qore sockets (denied ranges, allowed host patterns, ports and
      ranges, the default policy); it raises \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED naming the denied address, and returns the
      checked addresses as \c java.net.InetAddress objects that carry the host name; the Java code connects to one of
      them, never to the host name, so that a second lookup cannot return an address that was not checked (DNS
      rebinding)
    - <tt>QoreSandbox.checkFileAccess(path, write)</tt> and <tt>QoreSandbox.checkFileAccess(path, mode)</tt> check
      a path with the filesystem policy of %Qore \c File and \c Dir operations, and raise
      \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED
    - <tt>QoreSandbox.isSandboxed()</tt> tells whether a sandbox governs the caller, and
      <tt>QoreSandbox.restrictsFilesystem()</tt> whether a filesystem policy restricts it
    - <tt>QoreSandbox.checkCompilerClasspath(classpath)</tt> checks each entry of a classpath, the files that a wildcard
      selects and the entries that manifests name, as for the \c add-classpath module command, and returns the files
      to give a compiler.  For a directory, the directory itself is checked here; the files below it are checked when
      they are used (see below)

    <b>Classpath lookups:</b> every class and resource lookup below a classpath entry that restricted code added applies
    the filesystem policy of that code to the real path of the file, with symbolic links resolved, on any thread.  The
    policy is captured with the entry when it is added, so the result does not depend on the thread that makes the
    lookup, and a lookup that the JVM makes with no %Qore caller is checked in the same way.  A file that the policy
    denies, or that cannot be checked, behaves as a file that does not exist, and the following entries are still
    searched.  A policy that is bound to an entry is read for each lookup, so a change to it applies to the next lookup;
    a class that has already been loaded stays loaded.  A class loader to which no restricted code has added an entry
    makes no check.  A path is checked before it is opened, so a file that is replaced between the check and its use is
    not covered.  A real path that the JVM's file name encoding cannot represent exactly is treated as denied.  The
    encoding is read once when the module is initialized, as the file name encoding is fixed at startup; in a JVM that
    was started first, with %Qore as a library, Java code that runs before the module is initialized is part of the
    trusted host and must not change it.

    <b>Operator rules:</b>
    - whoever adds a classpath entry owns its policy.  An entry that restricted code adds carries that code's policy and
      fails closed
    - an entry that a trusted host deliberately adds, to the class loader of a sandboxed Program with the class loader's
      Java API or to the classpath that every Program shares, is a grant of the host: it is not also checked against
      the policy of the Program that owns the class loader, so that Program can load everything below it.  Add only
      what the Program may read
    - a policy is bound to an entry only if it restricts the filesystem when the entry is added.  An entry that was
      added before \c setSandboxManager(), or while the Program's sandbox restricted nothing, stays unchecked: setting or
      tightening the policy later does not apply to it.  Configure the filesystem policy of a Program before its
      classpath is added
    - on a JDK version for which manifest \c Class-Path handling has not been verified, what the manifests of trusted
      jars name is not found in a class loader that also has entries of restricted code (fail closed)

    <b>Compilers:</b> the Kotlin compiler validates the source and class names that it is given as Java binary names,
    keeps the files that it makes for them in its private temporary directory, and validates the names of the classes
    that it produces; the Java compiler keeps sources and classes in memory and makes no file for a name.  For a caller that a
    filesystem policy restricts, Java compiler options that name no file are accepted, a classpath and an output
    directory are checked against the policy and the compiler is given the files that were checked, annotation
    processing and the Java compiler's extensions from the classpath are turned off, and any other option is refused;
    use the \c add-classpath module command to add to the classpath.  For such a caller, the Kotlin compiler is given a
    private copy of the class files and Kotlin module files that every policy of the enclosing entries allows below each
    classpath directory that restricted code added, and its scripting plugin, which
    discovers script definitions on the classpath and runs their code, is turned off.  The copies that a Program has at
    one time are limited in number, files, bytes and directory entries visited, across all of its class loaders; a
    compile that would go over a limit fails, and the copies are removed when the compile ends, when their class
    loader is closed, and when the Program ends.  A copy that cannot be removed keeps its share of the limits, its
    removal is tried again at the next of these, and a removal that keeps failing is logged once, with no path; a compile
    does not raise this failure.  The copies are kept in one directory of the JVM that only its user can access, which
    is removed with its last copy if it is then empty; it stays, empty, and is used by the next copy if the JVM cannot read the temporary
    directory (for example mode 1733 of another owner, or 0300 owned by the JVM's user) or the JDK has no secure directory stream.  A retry only removes the directory it created: if that private directory no longer
    verifies (its type, owner, mode or identity changed), nothing below it is touched and its copies keep their share
    of the limits.  On a platform without POSIX file attributes or a secure directory stream in the JDK, a retry does
    not walk the directory, so such a copy keeps its share of the limits until the process ends.  A compile that
    needs a copy is refused unless the real path of the JVM's temporary directory is a directory owned by the JVM's numeric UID
    or by root that is not writable by group or others or that has the sticky bit; no user name is needed, and a compile
    is also refused if a UID cannot be read, which includes a Java runtime image without the \c jdk.security.auth
    module, or if a new copy would have another UID than its private directory.  The removal of such a refused copy is
    attempted; if it cannot be removed, it stays inside the private directory, and while that directory is the
    current one, compiles that need a copy are refused, with an error of their own that is logged once.  Once no copy is in flight, the next compile recovers if
    that directory is gone or is verified and empty; a leftover that never goes means refusal until the process
    restarts.  Compiles that make no copy are not affected.  Only on a platform without
    POSIX file attributes can this not be checked, and a copy is made.  Its parent directories and ACLs are not judged.
    These checks do not defend against Java code in the same JVM.  Once the module's shutdown has started, closing a class loader does
    nothing, so a copy of a compile that is still running is then removed only when that compile ends.  A process that
    ends with the \c exit() function while a compile is running does not remove that compile's copy, which stays in
    the JVM's temporary directory.  The Kotlin compiler also uses
    the host's %Qore and Kotlin jars and the entries that trusted code added.  A Kotlin script (\c kotlin_eval()) of such
    a caller is refused while a directory that restricted code added, directly or through a jar's manifest, is on its
    classpath, as the script compiler reads the files below it itself; use jars for that classpath.  The refusal
    covers only those directories: a jar is used as a whole once the policy has allowed it, and a directory with no
    bound policy (a grant of the host, or an entry added before a filesystem policy applied) does not refuse a script,
    which can then read all of it.  A compiler applies these rules to the caller that creates it: a
    compiler that trusted code creates keeps the options and classpath that it was given and does not check the files
    below its classpath directories, also when restricted code uses it, so do not hand one to restricted code.  A
    path is checked before it is opened, so a file that changes between the check and its use is not covered.  A
    classpath added with \c addClassPath() of a compiler or with \c addPath(), \c addWildcard() or
    \c addParentPath() of a class loader is checked against the same policy.  On a Java thread whose %Qore caller is
    not known, these operations follow the Java thread rule and its known limit, described above; a captured
    \c QoreSandbox.Policy does not change this.  These checks cover the names and options given to the compilers: Java code that a Program
    is allowed to run is not confined by them (see the rules below).

    The exceptions are thrown in Java as \c org.qore.jni.QoreExceptionWrapper (a \c RuntimeException), which becomes
    the original %Qore exception when it reaches %Qore code.  The policy allows callers that no sandbox restricts,
    though a check still raises an error for invalid arguments and on a thread whose policy cannot be determined (see
    below); the host name is resolved for them as well, so the code is the same in both cases.  In a system-policy capability
    (\c SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall()) the checks apply the system policy, as they do for %Qore sockets.

    <b>Java threads:</b> the static methods apply the policy of the %Qore caller of the current thread.  Work that
    Java runs on threads of its own (an executor, a connection pool, the I/O threads of a client library) has no
    %Qore caller: Java code captures the policy with <tt>QoreSandbox.capture()</tt> in the call from %Qore, before
    the work is handed over, and makes the checks through the returned \c QoreSandbox.Policy object, or runs the work
    with <tt>Policy.run()</tt>, <tt>Policy.call()</tt> or <tt>Policy.wrap()</tt>, which make the static methods use
    the captured policy on that thread.  A check made on a thread with no %Qore caller and no captured policy raises
    \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR instead of allowing the operation, so that a missing capture is an error and never a silent
    bypass.  <tt>QoreSandbox.restrictsFilesystem()</tt> differs on such a thread: it returns false until code that a
    sandbox governs has used Java in the process or the strict policy of Java threads is set, and raises
    \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR from then on.  %Qore code that Java calls back on a Java thread already runs under the sandbox
    of the code that handed it to Java (see @ref jni_callback_sandbox), so the checks it makes through Java use that
    sandbox.

    A captured \c QoreSandbox.Policy applies to every check made through it on any thread, also on a %Qore thread that
    runs other sandboxed code and inside a system-policy capability (\c SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall());
    the policy of the %Qore code that runs on the thread applies as well, so that the stricter one wins.

    @par Example:
    @code{.java}
import java.net.InetAddress;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.net.Socket;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox;

public class Fetcher {
    // called from Qore
    public static void fetch(ExecutorService executor, String host, int port) {
        // captured on the calling thread: the policy of the Qore code that called fetch()
        QoreSandbox.Policy policy = QoreSandbox.capture();
        executor.submit(policy.wrap(() -> {
            try (QoreSandbox.Policy p = policy; Socket s = new Socket()) {
                // on the executor thread: checked with the captured policy
                InetAddress[] addrs = QoreSandbox.checkNetworkAccess(host, port);
                // connect to the checked address, not to the host name
                s.connect(new InetSocketAddress(addrs[0], port));
                // ...
            }
            return null;
        }));
    }
}
    @endcode

    <b>Sandbox-aware Java code:</b> Java code may be declared to be sandbox-aware - safe to call from sandboxed
    %Qore code - only if it has been audited to follow these rules:
    - every network destination is checked with \c checkNetworkAccess() before it is used, and the connection is made
      to a returned address; a library that connects by host name (and resolves it again) or that connects to
      destinations it discovers itself (ex: the brokers that a Kafka bootstrap server returns, or redirects followed
      by an HTTP client) is configured not to, or each such destination is checked as well
    - every path is checked with \c checkFileAccess() with the access mode of the operation, as passed to the
      operation, before the file or directory is used
    - work run on other threads is checked with a policy captured in the call from %Qore
    - no I/O is made before the check; an operation that cannot be checked is not made

    A Java-backed data provider or module that has not been audited is not sandbox-aware, and must not be made
    available to sandboxed callers.  A data provider app declares this in its \c DataProviderAppInfo (\c DataProvider
    3.12 or later): \c external_io when its actions do I/O that the sandbox does not see, and \c sandbox_aware
    only after the audit; data provider consumers refuse sandboxed callers an app with \c external_io and without
    \c sandbox_aware (\c DataProviderActionCatalog::checkSandboxAccess(), \c APP-SANDBOX-ACCESS-DENIED).

    <b>Data provider apps of this module:</b>
    |!App|!Declaration|!The Java I/O that the sandbox does not see
    |Access|\c external_io|Jackcess opens the database file named by the action's path
    |Busylight, MQTT|\c external_io|the Paho client connects to the broker and to its failover URIs
    |Camel|\c external_io|Camel endpoints connect to and open what their URIs name
    |Flyway|\c external_io|the JDBC URL and the filesystem migration locations
    |JakartaJMS|\c external_io|the provider connects to the broker URL and loads the provider classpath
    |JasperReports|\c external_io|a report can load subreports, images and data from files and URLs
    |JdbcDP|\c external_io|the JDBC driver connects to the destinations of the URL
    |Kafka|\c external_io|the client connects to the bootstrap servers and to the brokers they return
    |OpcUA|\c external_io|the client connects to the server and the endpoints it returns
    |Avro, Email, Excel, ICS, ODP, ODS, ODT, PowerPoint, Tika, VCF, Visio, Word|\c external_io|Java only transforms the data, but every Java call is an uncontrolled API (\c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API)

    No app is declared \c sandbox_aware yet: each app with \c external_io lets a Java library connect to destinations
    that it resolves or discovers itself, or open files it finds itself, which these checks cannot cover until the
    app is changed to make them.

    The document apps (Avro, Email, Excel, ICS, ODP, ODS, ODT, PowerPoint, Tika, VCF, Visio and Word) read and write
    only the locations that %Qore opens for them (\c FileLocationHandler), and Java only transforms the streams it is
    given; the Excel and ODS apps also create temporary files of their own in the JVM's temporary directory, with
    names that no caller chooses.  They declare \c external_io as well, because a governing sandbox refuses the
    uncontrolled APIs (\c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API) that their Java calls are, also when library code makes them for a
    sandboxed caller (qore#5741): declared, they refuse sandboxed callers cleanly up front instead of failing in the
    middle of a call.

    The connection and data provider classes of the modules of the apps with \c external_io refuse sandboxed code in
    their constructors as well (\c APP-SANDBOX-ACCESS-DENIED), so that sandboxed code that loads such a module and
    creates its classes itself, without the data provider catalog, is refused too.  A connection that trusted code
    created refuses a sandboxed caller its I/O (\c get(), \c ping() and the other I/O methods of
    \c ConnectionProvider::AbstractConnection, which check the module of the connection's class), so a connection
    that a server uses on a sandboxed user's behalf is refused too.

    <b>Module commands, by their issuer:</b> built for module API 2.3 (%Qore with \c QoreModuleCommandContext), the
    module judges every command first by the restrictions of the code that issues it, which %Qore captures before it
    switches to the Program that the command applies to (a \c %module-cmd directive, \c Program::issueModuleCmd(), or
    a command replayed from a compiled program).  A sandboxed issuer is refused every command below; an issuer with a
    restricting parse option is refused the commands that the option forbids (\c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR).  With a %Qore
    that has the uncontrolled-API policy of sandboxes (qore#5741), code governed by a sandbox that allows uncontrolled
    APIs (\c SandboxManager::setUncontrolledApiPolicy(), for example a sandbox that a host attaches to trusted code only
    for its interrupts or limits) is not a sandboxed issuer here, nor in the checks of sandboxed code described below;
    the filesystem policy of the entries of a Program's classpath is still checked:
    |!Command|!Refused to a sandboxed issuer and to
    |\c import (also \c import \c pkg.*, whose classes are loaded when they are used)|\c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS
    |\c add-classpath, \c add-relative-classpath|\c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS, \c PO_NO_FILESYSTEM
    |\c define-class, \c define-pending-class, \c kotlin-eval|\c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS, \c PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC
    |\c global-add-classpath, \c global-add-relative-classpath, \c set-property, \c register-shared-class, \
      \c set-java-thread-policy|every restricting parse option: they change state that every Program shares
    |\c set-compat-types, \c mark-module-injected|none: they set flags of the Program the command applies to

    Code of a library module (a module from the module path) has no parse options, so only a sandbox restricts the
    commands it issues, also on behalf of a restricted caller: the provider modules of this module, which add
    classpaths at run time, can be used by a Program restricted only with parse options, and not by sandboxed code.
    Restricted code cannot issue a command into another, trusted Program at all (\c RUNTIME-COMMAND-ERROR).

    The commands that change state that every Program shares are process-wide by design, so they are refused to
    every restricted issuer, also to one whose parse options restrict something unrelated (ex: thread or process
    control) and whose sandbox trusts it with uncontrolled APIs: only code with no restricting parse option, or the
    code of a library module, may use them.  For example, the interface Programs of a server such as Qorus, which are
    trusted but have restricting parse options, may use \c set-compat-types, \c import, \c add-classpath and the
    \c define-class commands in their own code, but not \c set-property, \c register-shared-class or the \c global-*
    classpath commands; the server's own code, which has no restricting parse option, issues these into the
    interface Programs (\c Program::issueModuleCmd()).  A command issued into another Program also respects the
    target Program's \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS for the commands that change the JVM or define or run Java code (the
    \c global-* classpath commands, \c set-property, \c register-shared-class, \c define-class,
    \c define-pending-class and \c kotlin-eval), so a host issues them before it locks a Program down.  An
    \c import or \c add-classpath that a trusted issuer issues into a Program with \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS is
    allowed: it is the trusted code's decision, and the Program's own code still cannot use the Java classes, which
    are uncontrolled APIs.  The exported import functions (see below) judge the Program they import into as well,
    as they can run for code that is not the issuer of a module command.

    These rules apply to the module commands only: code with \c PO_NO_FILESYSTEM or \c PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC that may
    use Java classes can still change its class loader or the JVM through Java itself; only
    \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS, or a sandbox, keeps Java away from code.

    The exported functions \c jni_module_import() and \c jni_module_find_create_java_namespace(), which other modules
    (the \c python module's Java loader) call without a module command of this module, apply the rule of \c import:
    to the issuer of the module command that they run in, if any, and otherwise, with module API 2.3, to the code that
    calls into the loader, as for a module command issued by that code (for the code of a library module only its
    sandbox), or, before module API 2.3, to the declared parse options of the Program they import into
    (\c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS) and to a sandbox that governs the calling code.  The
    \c classpath option of the \c jdbc database driver applies the rule of \c add-classpath to the code that sets it,
    as for a module command issued by that code: its parse options and its sandbox, and for the code of a library
    module only its sandbox, also when a Program with \c %no-filesystem loaded the module.  These checks are also made
    with a module API before 2.3, where the \c jdbc option is judged by the parse options of the Program of the calling
    code and its sandbox.

    The \c jdbc \c classpath option is applied when a connection is opened, which can be on another thread and for
    other code than the code that set it (a \c DatasourcePool opens a connection on the thread that first needs one).
    With a %Qore library that records the code that sets each datasource option (qore#5741), <b>whoever configures
    the datasource decides</b>: the option is judged by the code that set it with \c Datasource::setOption(), with the
    options of a \c Datasource constructor or connection string, or, for every connection of a \c DatasourcePool, by
    the code that created the pool (copies keep it), and not by the code that opens the connection.  So a datasource
    whose classpath sandboxed or restricted code set is refused (\c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR) also when trusted code opens it,
    and the option of a trusted datasource or pool is not refused when a connection is opened on a restricted caller's
    thread.  The jdbc driver itself does not connect for code that a sandbox that does not allow uncontrolled APIs
    governs (\c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED, see the jdbc database driver below): a connection opened for sandboxed code, also
    by a trusted pool on its thread, and a connection of a datasource object that sandboxed code created or copied,
    whoever opens it, are refused before the option is judged.  The
    classpath entries are checked against the filesystem policy of the code that set the option, not of the code that
    opens the connection: the code that configures a datasource reads only what its own policy allows.  The module
    hash reports this with \c "jdbc_option_setter" in its \c "info" key; with an older %Qore library the option is
    judged by the code that opens the connection.

    <b>Module commands, by their target:</b> a module command also runs in the Program that it is issued in, also when
    trusted code issues it with \c Program::issueModuleCmd(), so it also follows that Program's sandbox and parse
    options.  Before module API 2.3 these are the only checks of the module commands; with module API 2.3, the parse
    options of the target are checked only for a command that another Program issues into it, as the issuer check
    already covers a Program's own commands (a library module's Program inherits the parse options of the code that
    loads it, which must not refuse the module's own directives):
    - the classpath that a sandboxed Program adds with the \c add-classpath and \c add-relative-classpath module
      commands, and with the \c classpath option of the \c jdbc database driver, is checked against its filesystem
      policy as a read: each entry is checked as its real path, with symbolic links resolved, and a denied entry
      raises \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED.  A wildcard entry is expanded once, and its directory and each file that it
      selects are checked, each as its real path, so that a symbolic link is judged by the file that it points to; the
      classpath is then added from the same expansion, so every entry that is given to the class loader is one of the
      paths that were checked, and nothing is resolved again.  If the policy denies any file that a wildcard selects,
      the whole entry is refused with \c JNI-CLASSPATH-ERROR, whose description names that file, as adding only some
      of its files would load another classpath than the one given (fail closed).  The jars and directories that the
      manifest \c Class-Path attributes of the classpath's jars name, which the class loader opens as well, are
      checked in the same way, recursively and each once: each is checked as the file that the class loader opens for
      it (a relative entry resolved against the location of the jar that names it, as the class loader resolves it),
      with symbolic links resolved, before its own manifest is read.  If the policy denies any of them, or a
      \c Class-Path entry cannot be checked (ex: it is not a file, a jar is not a regular file, a manifest cannot be
      read, or, before Java 22, a jar has a jar index), the whole classpath is refused with \c JNI-CLASSPATH-ERROR,
      naming the file.  The class loader still follows the manifests itself, so the classpath that is used is the one
      that was given, and every file of it is one that was checked.  That resolution follows the JDK's class loader,
      and it has been verified for JDK feature version 25; on any other JDK version, a classpath with a jar that has a
      manifest \c Class-Path attribute is refused with \c JNI-CLASSPATH-ERROR, naming the reason, while a classpath
      whose jars have no \c Class-Path attribute is checked and added as usual.  A denial refuses the whole classpath:
      nothing of it is added.  The policy is bound to the entries that are added, and applies to each file that the
      class loader then finds below them (see @ref jni_sandbox "Sandboxing Java I/O")
    - the commands that change state of the JVM that every Program shares (\c global-add-classpath,
      \c global-add-relative-classpath, \c set-property, \c register-shared-class, \c set-java-thread-policy) or
      that run Java code
      (\c kotlin-eval) raise \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR under a sandbox that does not allow uncontrolled APIs, also through
      a policy barrier or a system-policy capability, and in a Program with \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS, where the
      module's Java classes are not available
      either; so a sandboxed Program cannot make trusted Programs load its classes, or change how the JVM connects
      for them (ex: \c http.proxyHost, \c javax.net.ssl.trustStore).  The \c global-* classpath commands also raise
      \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR under a sandbox filesystem policy, including when that sandbox allows uncontrolled APIs and
      when trusted code issues the command into a Program that such a sandbox governs: an entry of the shared
      classpath carries no policy; a trusted host issues these commands in a Program of its own
    - \c define-class and \c define-pending-class raise \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR in a Program with
      \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS, as defining a class runs its static initializer, and a Program under a sandbox that
      does not allow uncontrolled APIs defines a class only in its own class loader: a class that belongs to another
      Program (ex: \c qoremod.MODULE.CLASS) cannot be defined by it

    <b>The jdbc database driver:</b> sandboxed code cannot open JDBC \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool connections
    for now: a \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool that uses the \c jdbc driver raises \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED when it
    connects or reconnects for code that a sandbox network policy restricts (the default network policy denies, or an
    allowed or denied IP range or an allowed port rule is set), whatever the destination and whatever the policy
    allows, before its options are processed (the \c classpath option loads jars and the JDBC drivers in them).  A sandbox manager that restricts no network access, such as an allow-all manager, does not refuse the driver
    (with the sandbox trust API, the rule below applies instead).  A
    connection that trusted code opened and then gave to sandboxed code stays usable until it must reconnect; trusted
    code that must not let sandboxed code use a database does not give it the \c Datasource.  The JDBC data provider
    (\c JdbcDataProvider) connects through the Java API itself, not through this driver, and is refused to sandboxed
    code as an \c external_io app.  Applying
    the sandbox network policy to the destinations of individual JDBC drivers is planned separately.  The JDBC driver opens its sockets in Java, so %Qore cannot apply the policy to them: a
    host name that was checked is resolved again by the driver, and drivers connect to destinations that they discover
    themselves (failover hosts, replicas, server redirects) or that have a syntax of their own for each driver (ex:
    Oracle TNS descriptors).

    <b>System-policy calls and caller provenance:</b> as with other policy checks, trusted code that connects under a
    policy barrier (\c SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy()) or from a capability
    (\c SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall()) is not refused.  A barrier masks only the sandbox of the callers on
    the stack, not the provenance of the code that it runs: a callback (a closure or call reference) that trusted code
    creates while it serves a sandboxed caller keeps that caller's sandbox, so under the barrier the core still
    applies the caller's sandbox to it, and the \c jdbc driver and the option checks refuse it as they would refuse
    the caller (as a %Qore socket or file is refused).  A callback that trusted code created beforehand, before it
    served the sandboxed caller (for example during its initialization), carries no such provenance, and a barrier
    around it runs it with the system policy.  A capability from \c SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall(), which
    trusted code registers before it runs sandboxed code, masks the provenance as well.

    <b>The rule with the sandbox trust API:</b> with a %Qore library that has
    \c QoreSandboxManager::trustsUncontrolledApis() (the jni module reports it with the \c sandbox_restricts_api key
    of its \c info hash in \c get_module_hash()), the \c jdbc driver and \c Jni::org::qore::jni::check_class_option()
    refuse code unless its governing sandbox trusts it with uncontrolled APIs
    (\c SandboxManager::setUncontrolledApiPolicy()), whatever the sandbox restricts: a sandbox that restricts nothing
    (an allow-all manager) but does not trust the code refuses them as well, because the Java code that they let run
    does I/O, uses threads and memory, and changes the state of the whole JVM, none of which the sandbox controls, as
    the %Qore library refuses such code every other uncontrolled API.  Trust only enables uncontrolled APIs: the
    network and filesystem policies of the sandbox still apply, and a %Qore library that checks the destinations of a
    connection against the sandbox of the code that chose it refuses the \c jdbc driver, which cannot report its
    destinations, for a connection chosen by code whose governing sandbox restricts network or filesystem access,
    whatever the sandbox trusts (\c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED; the library checks this before the driver is asked to
    connect, and whatever the jni module is built with).  The code that chose a connection is the code that created
    the datasource and that set its database name or connection values, not the code that opens it.  So the
    \c jdbc driver is refused unless the governing sandboxes of the code that opens the connection and of the code
    that chose it trust that code, and no network or filesystem policy of the governing sandbox of the code that
    chose it restricts it.  A host that governs trusted code with a sandbox only for its interrupts or limits sets
    \c SandboxManager::setUncontrolledApiPolicy() on it.  For code that more than one sandbox governs (a composition
    of sandboxes, for example when a callback or closure of one sandboxed %Program runs code of another), the
    sandbox trusts the code only if every member does.  The governing sandbox is
    resolved as for a policy check (\c QoreSandboxManagerHelper::Policy), so neither check applies inside
    \c SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() or a \c SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall() capability (see
    above): this is intended, as a system-policy call is an explicit, trusted capability in which trusted code acts
    as the system for its caller; such code must not open connections or use options on behalf of a sandboxed caller
    that the caller's own sandbox would not allow.  The \c classpath option of the \c jdbc driver, which loads jars,
    keeps its own rule (see @ref jdbc_option_classpath): it is judged by the restrictions of the code that set it, as
    a module command is, which a barrier does not lift.

    The values that choose the destination of a \c jdbc connection, the \c url option and the database name, can
    carry driver properties that make a JDBC driver instantiate classes, so they are judged by the code that set them
    as well, not only by the code that opens the connection (see @ref jdbc_option_url).

    Without the sandbox trust API, module code cannot judge a composition of sandboxes, so the \c jdbc driver is
    refused for code that a sandbox network policy restricts, as described above (this also refuses code that two
    allow-all managers govern together, whose composition reports a configuration that denies), and the options for
    code that any sandbox governs, also through a policy barrier or a system-policy capability.

    <b>Options that load Java code:</b> an option that names a Java class to load or instantiate, a classpath to add,
    or a value that Java compiles, deserializes or uses to select a component that loads classes, is refused with
    \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR to sandboxed code, before it is used: without the sandbox trust API also through a policy
    barrier or a system-policy capability, and with it, to code that its governing sandbox does not trust with
    uncontrolled APIs (see above).  The connections and data providers of these apps declare
    \c external_io, so their constructors refuse
    sandboxed code first (\c APP-SANDBOX-ACCESS-DENIED); the option checks apply where that refusal does not, for
    example to the static option checks that a module or a capability calls and to an object that trusted code
    created.  The run-time boundary is the sandbox (a \c Qore::SandboxManager), as for all module code: module code runs
    with its own Program's parse options, and a caller's parse options restrict it only when the caller is
    sandboxed, so a Program that is restricted only with parse options (ex: \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS) is not
    refused these options at run time when it uses a provider module that trusted code loaded.  A host that runs
    code it does not trust gives it a \c SandboxManager, or \c PO_NO_MODULES.  A restricted Program that loads such a
    provider module itself is refused when the module is loaded.

    The check is made for the caller, not for whoever set the value: these options are trusted configuration, and
    must not be taken from a less trusted source, such as connection settings that users edit, without validation.

    The provider modules of this module check:
    |!Module|!Options
    |JdbcDataProvider|\c classpath, \c driver_class, and a \c url with driver properties (any of \c ?, \c ;, \c =, \
      \c ( or \c &), which can name classes for the driver to load
    |JakartaJmsDataProvider|\c provider_classpath, \c connection_factory_class (always set, so sandboxed code \
      cannot use JMS)
    |KafkaDataProvider|\c ssl.engine.factory.class (except Kafka's \c DefaultSslEngineFactory, the connection's \
      default), \c sasl.client.callback.handler.class, \c sasl.login.callback.handler.class and \
      \c sasl.login.class (when set), the serializers and deserializers (except Kafka's own), and \
      \c sasl.jaas.config (except one entry for one of Kafka's own login modules), and \
      \c sasl.kerberos.kinit.cmd (except the connection's default, \c /usr/bin/kinit)
    |CamelDataProvider|the endpoint of a request, except a plain endpoint name, with no options or placeholders, of \
      the in-memory components \c direct, \c seda, \c log and \c mock; and \c CamelConnection::get(), which returns \
      the Camel context
    |JasperReportsDataProvider|the report template: its expressions are compiled and run as Java code, and a \
      compiled \c .jasper template is deserialized by Java
    |FlywayDataProvider|\c locations other than directories (ex: \c classpath:), and a \c url with driver \
      properties

    Modules check their own options with \c Jni::org::qore::jni::check_class_option().  With the sandbox trust API,
    code that a host governs with a sandbox that trusts it with uncontrolled APIs
    (\c SandboxManager::setUncontrolledApiPolicy()) may use these options; without it, any governing sandbox refuses
    them.

    The Kafka \c sasl.kerberos.kinit.cmd option names a command that the Kafka client runs in Java, outside of the
    process controls of %Qore.  It is trusted configuration: code that no sandbox governs can set it, so an application
    must not take its value from a less trusted source.

    @subsection jni_fork Child Processes Created with fork()

    Java cannot be used in a child process created with \c fork() after the JVM has been started (when the module
    is loaded): only the thread that called \c fork() exists in the child, while the threads of the JVM (its
    garbage collectors and compilers, and the module's own threads) remain in the parent.  In such a child:
    - every use of Java, including a Java object created in the parent, raises a \c JNI-FORK-ERROR exception
    - the deletion of a %Qore object or Program that uses Java, and the module's cleanup when the child exits or
      ends its script, neither call Java nor wait for the threads of the JVM
    - %Qore code that Java called and that called \c fork() must end the child with \c exit(): the child cannot
      return to the Java code that called it

    A child process that needs Java must start a new process (ex: by calling \c exec() in the child).

    @subsection jni_class_fields Java Class Fields to Qore Class Mappings

    Java fields are mapped to different %Qore class members according to the Java type according to the following
    table.

    <b>Java Field to Qore Class Member Mappings</b>
    |!Java|!Qore|!Qore Example
    |<tt><b>static final</b></tt>|%Qore class constants|@code{.py}MyClass::myField@endcode
    |<tt><b>static</b></tt> (non-final)|%Qore static class members|@code{.py}obj.myField@endcode
    |all others|normal %Qore class members|@code{.py}obj.myField@endcode

    For example:
    @code{.py}
System::out.println("testing...");
    @endcode

    @note Java field values are only stored in Java; they are not copied into the %Qore object.  Reading a field's
    member reads the field of the Java object, and assigning it (including compound assignments such as
    <tt>obj.count += 1</tt>) writes the field of the Java object, whether the object was created with a %Qore
    constructor or in Java; removing or deleting the member sets the field to \c null (or zero for a primitive
    field).  A field's member is not returned by the object's keys or hash conversions.  An access from outside the
    class to a field that %Qore code cannot access goes to the class's \c memberGate() method.

    A field's member, or the static class member of a mutable static field, is declared with the %Qore type of the
    field's Java type (see @ref jni_declared_value_types "Declared Types of Java Values"), and the value read from the
    field is checked against it.  A field of a reference type (an object or an array) can be \c null, so its type
    accepts no value: a \c java.lang.String field is declared as \c *string, a \c java.lang.Integer field as \c *int,
    and an \c int[] field as <tt>*softlist&lt;int&gt;</tt>; a \c null field is read as no value, and assigning no
    value sets the field to \c null.  A field of a primitive type cannot be \c null, so its type does not accept no
    value.  A \c java.lang.Character field is declared as \c *int, as a \c Character is converted to an integer, and a
    field of a class that the classes of values converted to %Qore values implement or extend (ex:
    \c java.lang.Number, \c java.lang.CharSequence, \c java.lang.Comparable, \c java.time.temporal.Temporal,
    \c java.util.Date) is declared as \c auto, as a value of such a class (ex: an \c Integer in a \c Number field) is
    converted to the %Qore value of its own class.

    A \c static \c final field is mirrored by a class constant holding the field's value, whose type is the type of
    the value; a \c null field is a constant with no value, declared with the field's type (ex: \c *string for a
    \c java.lang.String field).

    @subsection jni_access_mapping Java Access Mapping in Qore

    Java access modifiers are mapped directly to Qore access modifiers as in the following table.

    <b>Java to Qore Access Mappings</b>
    |!Java|!Qore|!Declared types
    |<b><tt>public</tt></b>|<b><tt>public</tt></b>|the %Qore types of the Java types
    |<b><tt>protected</tt></b>|<b><tt>private</tt></b> (or <b><tt>private:hierarchy</tt></b>)|the %Qore types of \
        the Java types
    |package-private (no modifier)|<b><tt>public</tt></b> (see @ref jni_package_private)|the %Qore types of the \
        Java types
    |<b><tt>private</tt></b>|<b><tt>private:internal</tt></b>|\c auto (see @ref jni_member_types)

    @subsection jni_package_private Package-Private Java Members

    A package-private Java member, one declared without an access modifier, is \c public in %Qore.  Calling it works
    when Java allows reflective access to it:
    - always for a class loaded from the classpath, as all its packages are open
    - for a class in a named module, such as the JDK's own modules, only if the module opens the class's package to
      the code of the \c jni module; otherwise the call raises a \c java.lang.IllegalAccessException.  A package is
      opened when the JVM starts, for example with
      <tt>QORE_JNI_JVM_ARGS=--add-opens=java.base/java.util=ALL-UNNAMED</tt>, or while it runs, by the module itself
      (\c Module.addOpens()) or by a Java agent.

    As this access can change while the JVM runs, a package-private member is always \c public in %Qore, whether or
    not it can be called when its class is imported.

    @subsection jni_member_types Declared Types of Java Members

    The %Qore class created for a Java class declares the types of its members' parameters, return values and fields
    with the %Qore classes created for the Java classes they name, which are imported with it.  A Java class that
    cannot be loaded or described there, typically because it needs an optional dependency that is not in the
    classpath, is declared as \c auto; this is reported once per process on \c stderr, naming the class and the
    Java exception, because a %Qore class built with such a type does not match the same class built where the
    dependency is available.

    The types of return values, fields and constants follow the rules in
    @ref jni_declared_value_types "Declared Types of Java Values": a value of a reference type can be \c null, so its type accepts no value (ex: \c *string for a
    \c java.lang.String return value).  The types of parameters follow the rules in
    @ref jni_declared_param_types "Declared Types of Java Parameters": a %Qore argument selects the variant Java would select for the value it is
    converted to.

    The parameter, return value and field types of \c private Java members are always declared as \c auto, and the
    classes they name are not imported for them: a private member cannot be called from %Qore code, only through the
    \c reflection module (ex: \c NormalMethod::call()), where Java allows reflective access to it (always for
    classes loaded from the classpath).  So an optional dependency that a library uses only
    internally is not reported either (for example the SQL Server JDBC driver's antlr dependency, needed only for
    its \c useFmtOnly option).  The \c reflection module therefore shows the variants of private Java methods and
    constructors, and private fields, with \c auto types; their arguments are converted to the Java parameter types
    when they are called.  A private Java method whose %Qore signature with \c auto types is the same as that of a
    non-private method of the same name is not added to the %Qore class.  Private \c static \c final fields are
    still constants with their values.

    @section jni_classloader Java Classloader

    There is a global classloader for all Java objects reachable without special classpath.  For all other objects,
    each %Qore @ref Qore::Program "Program" container has its own custom classloader provided by the \c jni module
    that supports a dynamic classpath.

    The %Qore @ref Qore::Program "Program"-specific dynamic classpath can be set with the \c import commands as
    documented in @ref jniimport.

    Explicit class loaders can also be used as in Java as in the following example:
    @code{.py}
    softlist<URL> urls = (new URL("file:///my/dir/my-api.jar"));
    URLClassLoader classLoader(urls);
    Class myClass = classLoader.loadClass("MyClass");
    @endcode

    @note The class loader supplied by this module, @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader, supports dynamic bytecode
    generation from Python and %Qore imports.

    Same-path classes projected as \c qore.<i>Namespace</i>.<i>Class</i> are Program-local by default because separate
    %Programs can legitimately provide different implementations at the same path.  Applications that pass objects
    of one semantically identical %Qore API class between sibling Program classloaders must register that exact Java
    binary name before loading it in any participating Program:
    @code{.py}
%module-cmd(jni) register-shared-class qore.MyApi.Request
    @endcode
    The first registering Program becomes the process-wide owner for that name, and registration by other Programs is
    idempotent.  Registering after two loaders have already defined the name raises an error because an existing JVM
    class-identity split cannot be repaired.  Do not register a name whose Qore definitions differ between Programs.

    @section jni_exceptions Java Exceptions in Qore

    If a @ref org.qore.jni.QoreException exception is thrown, then it is mapped directly to a Qore exception.

    Other Java exceptions are mapped to the %Qore @ref Qore::ExceptionInfo "ExceptionInfo" hash as follows:
    - \c err: always \c "JNI-ERROR"
    - \c desc: the full Java class name of the exception class; ex: \c "java.lang.RuntimeException"
    - \c arg: the exception object itself as a %Qore object wrapping the Java object

    Exception locations including call stack locations reflect the actual Java source location(s), and in such cases
    the \c lang attribute will be \c "Java". Negative Java source line numbers, including the native-method
    sentinel \c -2, are exposed as Qore's unknown-location sentinel \c -1. Available source line numbers are preserved.
*/
